------------------------ Taints & Tolerance ---------------------

# Taint
- Taint means in this context we explictly tell kubernetes cluster that this pod will not go on to the worker_node_1. 

Example :- 
root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster$ kubectl get nodes 
NAME                                 STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION
k8s-learning-cluster-control-plane   Ready    control-plane   38m   v1.35.0
k8s-learning-cluster-worker          Ready    <none>          38m   v1.35.0

- kubectl taint node  k8s-learning-cluster-worker prod=true:NoSchedule
- So then after u try to apply pod deployment (kubectl apply -f pod.yml) on this worker_node_1 so we will see the status is pending

# Tolerance
- Tolerance is the reverse example of taint :-
- Tolerance means in this context we explictly tell kubernetes cluster  worker_node_1 to tolerate the pod.


----------------------------------------------------------------------

------------------------ Taints & Tolerance ---------------------

- Node Affinity means if we want to specifically deploy the pod on the specific worker-node. So we should need to pass the configuration regarding that node like name, labels, region etc.

Example part from the manifest file :- 

spec:
  affinity:
    nodeAffinity:
      requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
        nodeSelectorTerms:
        - matchExpressions:
          - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
            operator: In
            values:
            - antarctica-east1
            - antarctica-west1

----------------------------------------------------------------------